function Point(x, y) {
this.x = x; // map M0 (empty) -> M1 {x}
this.y = y; // map M1 -> M2 {x, y}
}
const a = new Point(1, 2);
const b = new Point(3, 4); // follows the same transitions: same map M2
Two objects share a map only if they received the same properties in the same order from the same starting point. You can check this with V8's internal functions (debugging only, never in production):
// node --allow-natives-syntax shapes.js
const a = new Point(1, 2), b = new Point(3, 4);
%HaveSameMap(a, b); // true
const c = new Point(1, 2); c.z = 3;
%HaveSameMap(a, c); // false: one more transition
%HaveSameMap({ x: 1, y: 2 }, { y: 2, x: 1 }); // false: order matters
const d = new Point(1, 2); delete d.x;
%HasFastProperties(d); // false: dictionary mode
const f = new Point(1, 2); f.x = undefined;
%HasFastProperties(f); // true: shape unchanged
(Results from Node 22 / V8 12.4.)
For constructors and classes, V8 uses slack tracking: the first objects created get extra in-object space, and after a number of constructions V8 shrinks the allocation to what was actually used. Properties added long after construction end up in the out-of-line backing store — still fast, one extra pointer hop.

An inline cache (IC) is the feedback slot of one property access or call site. It remembers which maps it has seen and, for each, where the property lives.
IC state · Maps seen · What the fast path does · Optimisable?
Uninitialised · 0 · Nothing yet; the access has not run · No feedback, the optimiser inserts a deopt point
Monomorphic · 1 · One map check, then a load at a known offset · Best case
Polymorphic · 2–4 · A short chain of map checks · Yes, slightly slower per extra map
Megamorphic · more than 4 · Gives up per-site caching; uses a global stub cache (hash table) · TurboFan cannot inline the access
The cut-off is 4 maps in V8. A one-off measurement on Node 22 (V8 12.4, x86-64 cloud VM) with the script below shows the step clearly — about 26 ms with 1 shape, 47 ms with 4, and 114 ms with 5. Treat these as one run on one machine, not a benchmark result; the shape of the curve is what matters.
// poly.js — run each case in a fresh process: `for n in 1 2 4 5 8; do node poly.js $n; done`
const n = Number(process.argv[2] ?? 1);
function getX(o) { return o.x; }
const objs = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
const o = { x: i };
o['p' + (i % n)] = 0; // n distinct hidden classes, all with x first
objs.push(o);
}
let sum = 0;
const t0 = performance.now();
for (let r = 0; r < 20000; r++) for (let i = 0; i < objs.length; i++) sum += getX(objs[i]);
console.log(`${n} shapes: ${(performance.now() - t0).toFixed(0)} ms`, sum > 0);
Watch out: feedback belongs to the function, not to your benchmark case. Running several cases one after another through the same getX in one process gives nonsense, because the IC accumulates every shape it has ever seen. Isolate cases in separate processes.

When a guard in optimised code fails, V8 reconstructs the interpreter frame from the optimised frame (the compiler keeps metadata for this) and continues in Ignition or Sparkplug. The function can be optimised again later with the new feedback.
Kind · Trigger · Example
Eager · A guard fails inside optimised code · add was optimised for {x, y} Smis, then receives a string
Lazy · An assumption is invalidated elsewhere; the code is marked and deopts when control returns to it · Someone modifies a prototype or a constant field that the code depended on
Soft · The optimiser reached code that had no feedback yet · A branch that never ran before optimisation
Watching it happen:
// deopt.js — node --allow-natives-syntax --trace-deopt deopt.js
function add(p) { return p.x + p.y; }
%PrepareFunctionForOptimization(add);
add({ x: 1, y: 2 }); add({ x: 1, y: 2 });
%OptimizeFunctionOnNextCall(add);
add({ x: 1, y: 2 }); // optimised for map {x, y}
add({ x: 'a', y: 2, z: 1 }); // different map -> guard fails
[bailout (kind: deopt-eager, reason: wrong map): begin. deoptimizing … <JSFunction add …>, … <Code TURBOFAN>, opt id 0, bytecode offset 0, …]
The two fields to read are kind and reason. Common reasons: wrong map (shape changed), not a Smi / lost precision (number representation changed), out of bounds (array index past length), wrong call target, Insufficient type feedback.

How many distinct maps end up in list?
const list = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
const o = { id: i };
if (i % 2) o.name = 'odd';
if (i % 3 === 0) o.tag = 'three';
list.push(o);
}
Solution
List the property sequences: i=0 {id, tag}, i=1 {id, name}, i=2 {id}, i=3 {id, name, tag}, i=4 {id}, i=5 {id, name}. Distinct: {id}, {id, name}, {id, tag}, {id, name, tag} — four maps. A function reading o.id across this list is polymorphic (4 maps, the limit). Add one more optional field and it goes megamorphic. Fix: const o = { id: i, name: i % 2 ? 'odd' : null, tag: i % 3 === 0 ? 'three' : null }; gives one map.

Give the elements kind after each line: const a = new Array(3); · a[0] = 1; a[1] = 2; a[2] = 3; · const b = [1, 2, 3].map(x => x / 2); · const c = [1, 2, 3]; c.push(-0);
Solution
new Array(3) → HOLEY_SMI_ELEMENTS. Filling all three slots → still HOLEY_SMI_ELEMENTS (holey is permanent). b → PACKED_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS (map builds a fresh packed array; 0.5 and 1.5 are doubles). c.push(-0) → PACKED_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS, because -0 cannot be a Smi.
